<!--
  /composition — named slots (component <slot name> + consumer slot="x"),
  passing props to children, and event modifiers. Refs live on /data-binding.
-->
<column class="doc composition-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Composition</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Components compose through the platform's own seams: named slots for
    content, props for data down, and events for signals up. There's no slot
    API to learn and no ref-forwarding boilerplate — it's the web component
    model, made ergonomic.
  </p>

  <h2>Named slots</h2>
  <p>
    A component declares <code>&lt;slot name="…"&gt;</code> at the position it
    wants content, plus a bare <code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> for the default well.
    The consumer fills a named slot by putting <code>slot="…"</code> on a child;
    anything without a <code>slot</code> attribute lands in the default well.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.slots}></sui-codeblock>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.consumer}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Props down</h2>
  <p>
    Pass data to a child by setting attributes. The child declares each with
    <code>&lt;prop&gt;</code> and reads it as a signal — same syntax as any
    local signal. The parent owns the state; the child renders it.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.props}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Events + modifiers</h2>
  <p>
    Bind native events with <code>on:event</code>. Modifiers chain on the
    attribute to declare intent instead of writing the boilerplate by hand.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.events}></sui-codeblock>
  <ul class="mod-list">
    <li><code>prevent</code> — <code>event.preventDefault()</code> first</li>
    <li><code>stop</code> — <code>event.stopPropagation()</code> first</li>
    <li><code>self</code> — fire only when <code>event.target === event.currentTarget</code></li>
    <li><code>debounce:&lt;ms&gt;</code> — debounce the handler by N ms</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Data down, events up.</strong> Props flow into a child; the child
    reports back through events. Slots carry content across the boundary. Three
    seams, all standard, all in the template.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/styling">← Styling</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/tooling">Tooling →</a>
  </row>
</column>
